# Gantry — The game's state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely. It is the one value the
frame loop advances, and how it is organized is yours to decide.

The contract fixed over it is the debugging and automation surface
(`specs/instrumentation.md`). Every operation that surface carries is a reading
or a pose of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read,
honors what they pose, and returns to its title-screen values when the
surface's `reset` is called. This file states what it must carry, because the
rest of the specification and that surface's snapshot are written against these
facts.

## What the state carries

### The session

- The screen the game is showing, one of `title`, `howto`, `select`, `build`,
  `program`, `run`, and `results`, and the highlighted entry of whichever menu
  that screen shows, counted from `0`. The snapshot reports the second as
  `menuIndex`.
- The site the yard screens are showing, counted from `0`, which is `0` before
  any site has been opened.
- Per site: whether it has ever been cleared this session, and the best score
  recorded on it, a `cost` and a `time`, or none before the first clear. The
  two are set independently: a site can be marked cleared without a score and
  can carry a score without being marked cleared.
- Per site: the structure and the tape authored on it, which persist across
  visits for the session.

### The open site

- The loads standing in the yard, in the order the site lists them, each with
  its class (`crate`, `container`, or `drum`), its mass, its starting pose, and
  its target pose, a pose being a position and a yaw in degrees.
- The obstacles in the yard, each an axis-aligned box given as its minimum
  corner and its size on each axis.

Both are the open site's own copies, taken from the fixed set `specs/sites.md`
gives when the site is opened, so nothing the state holds points into that set
and a load posed on one site is not carried onto the next. The site's name,
envelope, anchors, budget, and par figures are not carried: they are the site's
own figures, read by the site index from that same fixed set.

### The structure being built

- The members: each with an id unique for the life of the site's structure, its
  two lattice nodes, and its material.
- The next member id to assign. Removing a member never gives its id back, so
  this does not follow from the members; emptying the structure whole returns
  it to `0` (`specs/instrumentation.md`).
- The slew ring by its base corner, or none.
- The nodes carrying a counterweight.

### The editor

- The selected build tool, one of `strut`, `cable`, `rail`, `ring`,
  `counterweight`, and `delete`.
- The pending first node of a member placement, or none.
- The undo history for the open site: the structure as it stood before each
  structure-changing edit since the site was opened, oldest first. The snapshot
  reports its depth as `historyDepth`.
- The result the static check is showing on the build screen, or none: the
  issues that would refuse a run, the cost, the budget, whether the structure
  stands, and each intact member's force and utilization in member-id order
  (`specs/structure.md`). It is the result the `check` action left, and it goes
  back to none as soon as the structure changes, so what is shown always
  describes the structure on screen.

### The tape

The ordered steps, each a move carrying one or more commands, at most one per
axis, or an `attach` or `release` action. A command names an axis, an absolute
target, and a rate.

### The camera and the pointer

- The orbit camera's yaw, pitch, and distance.
- The pointer position, in the stage's logical units, and whether a press is
  live; where the live press began, in those same units; and whether that press
  has passed `CLICK_SLOP`, which is what makes it an orbit drag rather than a
  click (`specs/controls.md`). The origin and that bit cannot be read back from
  the pointer's current position, so a press that began as a click and a press
  that has become a drag are told apart by what the state kept.

### The run

The run in progress, or the idle placeholder between runs:

- Its phase, one of `idle`, `running`, `cleared`, and `failed`, and its failure
  cause, or none, from the vocabulary `specs/statics.md` closes with.
- The tick count, from which the run clock is `tick / TICK_HZ` seconds, and the
  watch-speed index into `RUN_SPEEDS`.
- The tape step the run is on, counted from `0`, and whether that step is live:
  taken, and not yet complete. A move step is live from the tick that issues
  its commands until the tick that finds every one of its axes arrived; an
  action step is complete on the tick it is taken, so it is never live at the
  top of a tick (`specs/program.md`). Which of the two a tick does with the
  step it is on cannot be read off the axes, since a move whose commands have
  all cleared and a move not yet issued leave the axes alike.
- Each of the four axes: its value, its signed rate, and its live command's
  target and rate, or none.
- The pivot the cable hangs from, at the most recent tick's geometry. The
  pendulum reads it as the previous pivot on the tick after
  (`specs/rigging.md`), and it cannot be recomputed from the axes once a rail
  has broken, because the track it was taken along is gone.
- The pendulum bob's position and velocity.
- The attached load's index, or none.
- Each load's run state, index-aligned with the open site's loads: its phase,
  one of `waiting`, `attached`, `placed`, and `lost`, its lift point's current
  position, and its yaw.
- The latest solve's force and utilization per intact member, in member-id
  order.
- The ids of the members broken so far this run, in the order they broke.

### The frame

- `muted`, the game's readable copy of the runtime's mute bit, refreshed on
  every update.
- `simTime`, accumulating every update's delta time in seconds, whatever the
  screen.

## The idle run

Between runs the run carries its idle placeholder: phase `idle`, no cause, a
zero tick, step index, and speed index, no live step, the four axes at the
run-start posture `specs/program.md` fixes with no command and zero rate, a
zero pivot, a bob at the origin with zero velocity, no attachment, and an empty
load, force, and broken list. Every field is present whatever the screen, so
the snapshot never reports a gap.

## What is derived

These are read off the facts above rather than kept beside them, so they answer
to the state as it stands.

| Fact | Read from |
| --- | --- |
| The crane's cost | The structure's members, ring, and counterweights (`specs/structure.md`) |
| The readiness issues | The structure and the site (`specs/structure.md`) |
| The intact members | The structure's, less the run's broken ids |
| The rail track, its origin, and its length | The intact rail members (`specs/structure.md`) |
| The rotated arm, the trolley point, and the pivot within a tick | The slew and trolley values and the structure (`specs/statics.md`) |
| The run clock | `tick / TICK_HZ` seconds |
| Whether a site is open | Site `0` is open, and site `n + 1` is open once site `n` is cleared (`specs/ui.md`) |
| The site's name, envelope, anchors, budget, and par | The site index, against `specs/sites.md` |
| The node or member a click would take | The pointer position and the camera (`specs/controls.md`) |
| Whether the pointer's press is a click or a drag so far | The press origin and the drag bit above |

## The contract

- The state is the whole of the authoritative game. Every value the game
  carries from one frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a
  module-level variable or a closure.
- Every fact above is present from the moment the game has initialized, so a
  frame never finds one missing.
- What you add beside them holds either data you can rebuild from them, or the
  bookkeeping of what the build presents: the sounds it plays and the picture
  it draws, which no figure this specification fixes depends on. A `reset`
  restores the facts above to their title-screen values, as
  `specs/instrumentation.md` states, so those are the whole of the
  authoritative state.
- Rendering reads the state and writes nothing, and whether an edit is accepted
  and what a tick does are decided from the state and the site alone.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is a value the engine holds and every frame replaces: `update`
and the debug surface's poses are handed it as `DeepReadonly<GantryState>` and
return the next `GantryState`, so a frame builds the next state from the
current one rather than writing into it. `SiteLoad` and `Obstacle` are the site
table's own record types, which `src/constants.ts` exports beside `SITES`, so
the open site's loads and obstacles are held in the shape the table gives them.

```ts
/** A position, in world units; a lattice node carries lattice coordinates. */
export interface Vec3 {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  z: number;
}

export type MaterialName = "strut" | "cable" | "rail";

/** One member. `id` is unique for the life of a site's structure. */
export interface Member {
  id: number;
  a: Vec3;
  b: Vec3;
  material: MaterialName;
}

/** What the player has built on a site. */
export interface Structure {
  members: Member[];
  /** The next member id to assign; a removed member's is never given back. */
  nextMemberId: number;
  /** The slew ring, by its base corner (specs/structure.md), or none yet. */
  ring: { corner: Vec3 } | null;
  /** The nodes carrying a counterweight. */
  counterweights: Vec3[];
}

export type AxisName = "slew" | "trolley" | "hoist" | "grip";

export interface Command {
  axis: AxisName;
  target: number;
  rate: number;
}

/** One tape step: a move of one or more axes, or an action. */
export type Step =
  | { kind: "move"; commands: Command[] }
  | { kind: "action"; action: "attach" | "release" };

/** What the open site puts in the yard (specs/sites.md). */
export interface SiteState {
  loads: SiteLoad[];
  obstacles: Obstacle[];
}

/** A structure's readiness issue (specs/structure.md). */
export type ReadinessIssue =
  | "no-ring"
  | "no-rail"
  | "invalid-rail"
  | "disconnected-members";

/** What would refuse a run: a readiness issue, or an empty tape. */
export type StartIssue = ReadinessIssue | "empty-program";

/** One member's solved force and utilization (specs/statics.md). */
export interface MemberForce {
  id: number;
  force: number;
  utilization: number;
}

/** What the static check reported (specs/structure.md). */
export interface CheckResult {
  issues: StartIssue[];
  cost: number;
  budget: number;
  stable: boolean;
  members: MemberForce[];
}

/** One axis during a run. `command` is null when no command is live on it. */
export interface AxisState {
  value: number;
  rate: number;
  command: { target: number; rate: number } | null;
}

export type LoadPhase = "waiting" | "attached" | "placed" | "lost";

/** One load during a run, index-aligned with the open site's loads. */
export interface LoadState {
  phase: LoadPhase;
  /** The lift point's current position (specs/world.md). */
  pos: Vec3;
  yaw: number;
}

export type RunPhase = "idle" | "running" | "cleared" | "failed";

export type FailCause =
  | "collapse"
  | "ring-overload"
  | "cable-snap"
  | "structure-struck-obstacle"
  | "load-struck-obstacle"
  | "load-struck-ground"
  | "attach-missed"
  | "release-misplaced"
  | "command-out-of-range"
  | "loads-unplaced";

/** The run in progress, or the idle placeholder between runs. */
export interface RunState {
  phase: RunPhase;
  /** The failure cause; null unless phase is "failed". */
  cause: FailCause | null;
  /** Ticks since the run started; the run clock is tick / TICK_HZ. */
  tick: number;
  /** Index into RUN_SPEEDS. */
  speedIndex: number;
  /** The tape step the run is on; the tape's length once all are done. */
  stepIndex: number;
  /** Whether that step is live: taken, and not yet complete. */
  stepLive: boolean;
  axes: {
    slew: AxisState;
    trolley: AxisState;
    hoist: AxisState;
    grip: AxisState;
  };
  /** The point the cable hangs from, at the latest tick's geometry. */
  pivot: Vec3;
  /** The pendulum bob: the hook point (specs/rigging.md). */
  bob: { pos: Vec3; vel: Vec3 };
  /** The attached load's index, or null. */
  attached: number | null;
  loads: LoadState[];
  /** The latest solve's, in member-id order, over the members still intact. */
  forces: MemberForce[];
  /** The ids of members broken so far this run, in the order they broke. */
  broken: number[];
}

/** A cleared site's recorded score. */
export interface Score {
  cost: number;
  time: number;
}

/** The orbit camera (specs/controls.md). */
export interface Camera {
  yaw: number;
  pitch: number;
  dist: number;
}

/** The pointer, as the frame read it (specs/controls.md). */
export interface PointerState {
  /** The pointer position, in the stage's logical units. */
  x: number;
  y: number;
  /** Whether a press is live. */
  down: boolean;
  /** Where the live press began, in those same units. */
  pressX: number;
  pressY: number;
  /** Whether the live press has passed CLICK_SLOP, so it is an orbit drag. */
  dragging: boolean;
}

export type Screen =
  | "title"
  | "howto"
  | "select"
  | "build"
  | "program"
  | "run"
  | "results";

export type Tool =
  | "strut"
  | "cable"
  | "rail"
  | "ring"
  | "counterweight"
  | "delete";

export interface GantryState {
  screen: Screen;
  /** The highlighted entry of the current screen's menu. */
  menuIndex: number;
  /** The site the yard screens show, counted from 0. */
  siteIndex: number;
  /** Per site: whether it has been cleared this session. */
  cleared: boolean[];
  /** Per site: the best recorded score, or null with none recorded. */
  best: (Score | null)[];
  /** Per site: the structure and tape authored on it, kept across visits. */
  sites: { structure: Structure; program: Step[] }[];
  /** The open site's loads and obstacles. */
  site: SiteState;
  /** The selected build tool. */
  tool: Tool;
  /** The held first node of a member placement, or null. */
  pendingNode: Vec3 | null;
  /**
   * The undo stack for the open site: the structure as it stood before each
   * structure-changing edit since the site was opened, oldest first.
   */
  history: Structure[];
  /** The check result the build screen is showing, or null. */
  checkResult: CheckResult | null;
  camera: Camera;
  pointer: PointerState;
  run: RunState;
  /** The engine's mute bit, mirrored every update. */
  muted: boolean;
  /** Accumulated delta time of every update, in seconds. */
  simTime: number;
}
```

## The type aliases

`MaterialName` names the three materials `specs/structure.md` gives and
`AxisName` the four axes `specs/program.md` gives. `Screen` is the top-level
state machine and every build carries all seven; `Tool` names the six build
tools. `ReadinessIssue` is the four issues `specs/structure.md` names and
`StartIssue` adds the one a tape can raise, so `StartIssue` is exactly the set
a refused start lists. `LoadPhase` and `RunPhase` name where a load and a run
stand, and `FailCause` is the closed vocabulary of failures `specs/statics.md`
collects.

## `Structure`

What is built on one site, and `{ members: [], nextMemberId: 0, ring: null,
counterweights: [] }` on a site nothing has been built on.

- `members` — every member, each carrying the id it was assigned.
- `nextMemberId` — the id the next member takes. Removing a member never gives
  its id back, so this does not follow from `members`: removing the
  highest-numbered member leaves it where it is. Emptying the structure whole
  returns it to `0` (`specs/instrumentation.md`).
- `ring` — the slew ring by its base corner, from which its eight flange nodes
  and its slew axis follow (`specs/structure.md`), or `null` with no ring.
- `counterweights` — the nodes carrying one, at most one per node.

## `SiteState`

What the open site puts in the yard, copied out of the fixed set
`specs/sites.md` gives when the site is opened.

- `loads` — the site's loads, in the order that file lists them, each with its
  class, its mass, the pose it starts at, and the pose it is wanted at. The
  build screen draws them at `from` and their pads at `to`, and a run's
  `run.loads` is index-aligned with this list.
- `obstacles` — the site's obstacles, each the axis-aligned box it fills.

The state owns these all the way down: opening a site copies the site's loads,
their poses, its obstacles, and their corners and sizes, so nothing here points
into the fixed set and posing a load on one site leaves every other site as it
was. The site's name, envelope, anchors, budget, and par figures are not
carried; they are read by `siteIndex` against `specs/sites.md`.

## `CheckResult`

What the `check` action last reported, and what the build screen is showing
until the structure changes (`specs/structure.md`, `specs/ui.md`). It is
`null` whenever nothing is showing.

- `issues` — what would refuse a run, empty when nothing would.
- `cost`, `budget` — the crane's cost and the site's budget.
- `stable` — whether the structure stands.
- `members` — each intact member's force and utilization, in member-id order,
  and empty where an issue left the structure unsolved.

## `PointerState`

Where the pointer is and what the press being held has done so far, as the
frame read it from the engine.

- `x`, `y` — the pointer position in the stage's logical units, the units a
  pick radius is measured in.
- `down` — whether a press is live.
- `pressX`, `pressY` — where that press began, which decides whether it is
  still a click. They rest where the last press began and are read while `down`.
- `dragging` — `false` whenever no press is live, and `true` from the move
  that carries a live press past `CLICK_SLOP` (`6`) until it is released. That
  move is the one that makes the press an orbit drag rather than a click, and
  once it is a drag it stays one.

## `RunState`

The run in progress, and the idle placeholder whenever none is.

- `phase` — `"idle"` between runs, `"running"` while the tape plays,
  `"cleared"` and `"failed"` once it has ended.
- `cause` — the failure cause, and `null` unless `phase` is `"failed"`.
- `tick` — ticks since the run started. The run clock is `tick / TICK_HZ`
  seconds.
- `speedIndex` — the watch speed, as an index into `RUN_SPEEDS`.
- `stepIndex` — the tape step the run is on, counted from `0`, which reaches
  the tape's length once every step is complete. The run screen shows it as
  `step m / n` with `m` one greater (`specs/ui.md`).
- `stepLive` — whether the step at `stepIndex` has been taken and is not yet
  complete. A move step is live from the tick that issues its commands until
  the tick that finds every one of its axes arrived; an action step is taken,
  executed, and complete on one tick, so it is never live at the top of one
  (`specs/program.md`). A move whose commands have all cleared and a move not
  yet issued leave the axes alike, so this does not follow from them.
- `axes` — the four axes, each carrying its value, its signed rate, and the
  command live on it, or `null` with none.
- `pivot` — the point the cable hangs from, at the geometry of the most recent
  tick. The pendulum reads it as `P_prev` on the tick after
  (`specs/rigging.md`), and a rail breaking takes away the track it was
  measured along, so it does not follow from the axes.
- `bob` — the pendulum bob's position and velocity. The hook point and any
  attached load's lift point are both `bob.pos`.
- `attached` — the index of the load on the hook, or `null`. One load is
  attached at a time.
- `loads` — one entry per load of `site.loads`, in the same order.
- `forces` — the latest solve's force and utilization for each member still
  intact, in member-id order.
- `broken` — the ids of the members broken so far this run, in the order they
  broke.

The idle placeholder is `phase` `"idle"`, `cause` `null`, `tick`, `speedIndex`,
and `stepIndex` `0`, `stepLive` `false`, the four axes at the run-start posture
`specs/program.md` fixes with zero rate and no command, `pivot` and both parts
of `bob` the zero vector, `attached` `null`, and `loads`, `forces`, and
`broken` empty.

## `GantryState`

The whole of Gantry's state. Every field is present from the moment
`initialize` returns, and every one is plain data: numbers, strings, booleans,
and containers of them, so a site is posed by building a state that differs in
the fields posed and read back by reading them.

- `screen` — the screen currently shown.
- `menuIndex` — the highlighted entry of whichever menu `screen` shows.
- `siteIndex` — the site the build, program, and run screens show, counted
  from `0`, and `0` before any site has been opened.
- `cleared` — one entry per site, `true` once that site has been cleared this
  session.
- `best` — one entry per site: the best score recorded on it, or `null` with
  none. It is set apart from `cleared`, so a site may carry one without the
  other.
- `sites` — one entry per site: the structure and tape authored on it, which
  stay as they are while other sites are played and while runs are made.
- `site` — the open site's loads and obstacles.
- `tool` — the selected build tool.
- `pendingNode` — the first node of a member placement being held, or `null`.
- `history` — the undo stack for the open site, oldest first. `undo` pops it,
  and it empties when a site is opened. The snapshot reports its depth as
  `historyDepth`.
- `checkResult` — the check result the build screen is showing, or `null`.
- `camera` — the orbit camera's pose, which persists across the three yard
  screens and returns to its start pose when a site is opened.
- `pointer` — the pointer as of this frame, refreshed from the engine on every
  update.
- `run` — the run in progress, or its idle placeholder.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the engine's mute bit, which every
  `update` carries into the state it returns. The engine owns muting, and this
  field is what the debug surface reports.
- `simTime` — accumulated simulation time, in seconds. Every `update` adds its
  `dt`, whatever the screen, the menus included.

## What a site opening does

Opening a site sets `siteIndex`, keeps that site's stored structure and tape,
fills `site` with copies of that site's loads and obstacles, empties `history`,
clears `pendingNode` and `checkResult`, returns the camera to its start pose,
and leaves `run` at its idle placeholder. `initialize` builds the title-screen
state with site `0` opened this way: the `title` screen with `menuIndex` `0`,
every site uncleared with no recorded score and an empty structure and tape,
the `strut` tool, and `simTime` `0`.

## What is derived

Ten facts the game shows are read off the fields above rather than kept beside
them, so they answer to the state as it stands.

| Fact | Read from |
| --- | --- |
| The crane's cost | `sites[siteIndex].structure` (`specs/structure.md`) |
| The readiness issues | That structure and the site (`specs/structure.md`) |
| The intact members | That structure's members, less `run.broken` |
| The rail track, its origin, and its length | The intact rail members (`specs/structure.md`) |
| The rotated arm, the trolley point, and the tick's pivot | `run.axes` and the structure (`specs/statics.md`) |
| The run clock | `run.tick / TICK_HZ` seconds |
| Whether a site is open | Site `0`, and site `n + 1` once site `n` is cleared (`specs/ui.md`) |
| The site's name, envelope, anchors, budget, and par | `siteIndex`, against `specs/sites.md` |
| The node or member a click would take | `pointer` and `camera` (`specs/controls.md`) |
| The bob's previous velocity | `run.bob.vel` as the tick found it |

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
- `initialize` builds the whole state in one go, so every field is present by
  the time a frame can observe it, and none of them is optional.
- The state handed to `update` is the whole of the game, and the state `update`
  returns is the whole of the next frame. Every value the game carries from one
  frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level variable or
  a closure.
- Fields you add hold either data you can rebuild from the declared ones, or
  the bookkeeping of what the build presents: the sounds it plays and the
  picture it draws, which no figure this specification fixes depends on. A
  `reset` restores the declared fields to their title-screen values, as
  `specs/instrumentation.md` states, so those fields are the whole of the
  authoritative state.
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<GantryState>`, to
  `update`, to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation of
  the debug surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new
  `GantryState` built from the one they received; `render`, the sources, and
  `snapshot` return no state.
{{else}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is the world's game state: a class extending the engine's
`GameState`, named by the game mode as its `gameStateClass`, so the engine
builds one instance of it when the world opens and `engine.world.state` is that
instance. Wherever this specification writes `state.foo`, it means a field of
that instance.

The game runs in one world for the whole session. The game definition registers
a single level, the game never opens another, and every screen is a value of
`screen`, so the state below is built once and lives until the page closes. The
framework's states are live objects: a tick writes the fields it advances in
place, and each field initializer below is that field's title-screen value, the
same value the debug surface's `reset` restores. `GameState` comes from the
engine's package; `SITES`, `SITE_COUNT`, `HOIST_START`, `CAMERA_START_YAW`,
`CAMERA_START_PITCH`, and `CAMERA_START_DIST` come from `src/constants.ts`, and
so do `SiteLoad` and `Obstacle`, the site table's own record types, so the open
site's loads and obstacles are held in the shape the table gives them.

